yes(485,'D','Saponification is alkaline hydrolysis of an ester. An alkanoate ester reacts with sodium hydroxide to form a sodium carboxylate and an alcohol: RCOOR′ + NaOH → RCOONa + R′OH. Fats give soap and glycerol because they are glycerol esters.',[CARB,REB,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/13461?c_page=7&exam_type=jamb&page=168','https://www.schoolngr.com/classroom/chemistry/13580'],year=1993,question='Which reaction represents saponification?',options=['Carboxylic acids reacting with sodium hydroxide','Alkanoate esters reacting with acids','Alkanoate esters reacting with alcohols','Alkanoate esters reacting with sodium hydroxide'],audit='Actual source is1993Q49 on PDF40, not1994. Stable ID retained. Previously held after TeamBoma3947 redirected. Now recovered from readable full Myschool13461 and SchoolNGR13580 pages, both explicitly1993 with all four options; C is alkanoates with alcohols. Explained general ester hydrolysis without claiming all esters yield glycerol.')
yes(486,'C','One carbon 2s orbital and three carbon 2p orbitals form four sp³ hybrid orbitals in the bonding model for methane. Each overlaps with a hydrogen 1s orbital to form a C–H sigma bond.',[ORG],question='Which carbon atomic orbitals are hybridised for bonding with hydrogen in methane?',options=['1s and 2p','1s and 2s','2s and 2p','2s and 3p'],audit='Clarified source mixed-with-hydrogen wording to distinguish hybridisation within carbon from subsequent overlap with hydrogen.')
